💡 **SUGGESTION: the bounded 500-job scenario runs twice in the same test**

`statusLineCount(500, maxRetainedExitedJobs: 100)` already executes the full 500-start scenario inside `statusText` and keeps only the line count; the very next expect re-runs the identical scenario just to pin the summary text. One run can serve both assertions:

```dart
final bounded = await statusText(500, maxRetainedExitedJobs: 100);
expect(bounded.split('\n').length, 2 + 20 + 1);
// Non-vacuous pruning pin: the summary reports the RETAINED count —
// if the cap never pruned, this would read 500 exited total.
expect(bounded, contains('(100 exited total'));
```

This halves the remaining allocation churn in the exact test gh-1789 bounded — the file's whole point — without losing either assertion or any coverage (the pruning pin and the listing-cap count observe the same deterministic registry state).

